course: dictionary
lesson: 5945
title: Massive and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Massive is one word with three meanings, so context tells you which one you need. Look at the words around it. «The massive dog knocked over the bin.» A dog that knocks over a bin is big and heavy, so massive means very big and heavy.
explain: «He made a massive mistake in the test.» A mistake cannot weigh anything, so here massive means very large in amount or degree. Try swapping in "very big" and see if the sentence still makes sense. It does, so you have chosen the right meaning.
explain: In British and Australian slang, massive can mean very popular or important, so think about who is talking and where. Writers also use massive to exaggerate, as in «a massive thank you». A precise word like sticky or lopsided paints a clearer picture than piling up big, huge and massive.
fact: Massive means very big and heavy, or very large in amount or degree.
fact: «Vesta is the second most massive asteroid.» Here massive means having a lot of mass, or matter.
fact: «Jupiter is more than twice as massive as all the other planets put together.» Scientists use massive to compare how much matter objects have.
fact: In British and Australian slang, massive means very popular or important.
fact: Massive is an adjective, so it can change to more massive and most massive.
exercise: «The massive wave crashed onto the beach.» What does massive mean here? | Very kind | Very quiet | *Very big and heavy | Very cheap
exercise: «Jupiter is more than twice as massive as all the other planets put together.» What does massive mean in this science sentence? | Very popular | *Having a lot of mass, or matter | Very short | Very quiet
exercise: Mum says «a massive thank you». What does massive mean? | Very old | *Very big, in amount or degree | Very wet, like a rainy day | Very heavy, like a rock
test:
«The massive box was too heavy to lift.» What does massive mean in this sentence? | Very popular | *Very big and heavy | Very quiet | Very old
Kai says, «That new game is massive, everybody is playing it.» What does massive mean in British slang? | Very wet | Very short | *Very popular | Very old
exam:
Why is one precise word like «lopsided» better than piling up big, huge, enormous and massive? | It is longer and sounds more grown up | It means the same as huge | It is easier to spell | *It paints a clear picture for the reader
